.TH smi-pki-do-client-signing "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-do-client-signing \- Sign a received client certificate request with the Intermediate CA
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-do-client-signing
.I DIRECTORY CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Signs \fIDIRECTORY\fB/\fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.csr\fR with the Intermediate CA \fBca/\fIROOT_DOMAIN\fB.intermediate.crt\fR and \fB.intermediate.priv.key\fR, the root domain given to \fBsmi-pki-ca-create\fR(1).
.PP
Before signing, the Intermediate CA must exist and the request must pass \fBsmi-pki-verify-client-csr\fR(1): a valid self-signature and \fICLIENT_NAME\fR as its only Common Name.
The certificate gets the client extensions of \fBsmi-pki-client-extensions\fR(1), CA:FALSE and extendedKeyUsage=clientAuth, whatever the request asked for, and is valid for 365 days (\fBCLIENT_CERT_VALIDITY_DAYS\fR in \fBpki.sh\fR).
The subject is taken from the request.
.PP
Writes \fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.crt\fR, \fB.client.ext\fR and \fB.client.chain.crt\fR, the certificate followed by the Intermediate CA certificate, which is the file to send back.
It is a thin command line wrapper over the matching function in the file based PKI library
\fBpki.sh\fR, so each step of building a CA and signing certificates can be run and inspected
on its own. All material is passed as file names; the command fails if the underlying OpenSSL
operation fails.
.SH MUTUAL TLS WORKFLOW
.IP 1. 4
The client runs \fBsmi-pki-do-client-cert-request\fR \fIDIRECTORY CLIENT_NAME\fR on its own machine,
with the agreed \fICLIENT_NAME\fR, and sends only \fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.csr\fR. The private key never
leaves the client.
.IP 2. 4
The CA side saves the request as \fIDIRECTORY\fB/\fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.csr\fR, may inspect it with
\fBsmi-pki-verify-client-csr\fR, and signs it with \fBsmi-pki-do-client-signing\fR
\fIDIRECTORY CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN\fR.
.IP 3. 4
The CA side sends back \fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.chain.crt\fR, and the Root CA certificate
\fBca/\fIROOT_DOMAIN\fB.crt\fR when the client does not have it yet.
.IP 4. 4
The server trusts the CA, for example nginx \fBssl_client_certificate\fR with the Root CA and
Intermediate CA certificates and \fBssl_verify_client on\fR; the client connects with its private
key and the chain file.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
smi-pki-do-client-signing /var/opt/pki acme-billing gintra
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The certificate was signed.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong usage, the Intermediate CA was not found, the request failed the check, or an OpenSSL step failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
